HypXRland stack on Omedora 4
============================

Installing hypxrland-omedora pulls in this complete runtime stack and keeps the
ordinary Omedora Hyprland session installed as a fallback. Select "Omedora XR"
at the display manager; it loads ~/.config/hypr/hyprland-xr.lua.

The RPMs do not overwrite files in your home directory. A source-tree setup
that hard-codes development binaries should be changed to the packaged paths:

  hypxrvoicectl      /usr/bin/hypxrvoicectl
  hypxrva-watcher    /usr/bin/hypxrva-watcher
  hypxrpaper         /usr/bin/hypxrpaper
  hypxrcompose       /usr/bin/hypxrcompose
  HUD daemon         /usr/bin/hypxrhud (D-Bus activated)
  HUD battery        /usr/bin/hypxrhud-battery
  HUD keystrokes     /usr/bin/hypxrhud-keys
  HUD command ticker /usr/bin/hypxrhud-cmdlog
  hyprctl ticker shim /usr/share/hypxrhud/shim/hyprctl
  WiVRn runtime      /usr/share/openxr/1/openxr_wivrn.json

The Omedora XR launcher selects the packaged hypxrva driver automatically.
WiVRn pairing and component configuration remain per-user state.
The package does not alter firewalld policy; WiVRn uses TCP and UDP port 9757.
hypxrcompose validates and renders WiVRn's recorded .hypxrtake bundles offline.
Fedora's packaged hypxrcompose defaults to OpenH264; see
/usr/share/doc/hypxrcompose/hypxrcompose-fedora.md for codec and stereo-metadata
limitations.

HypXRland now supports both Lua and classic hyprlang XR declarations. Omedora XR
uses ~/.config/hypr/hyprland-xr.lua as its entry point, and
the hypxrland-legacy-config package keeps the immutable classic default *.conf
tree beside Quattro's Lua files. The stable Omedora session continues using
Lua. Advanced users can use /usr/share/doc/hypxrland/contrib/hyprland-xr.lua as
a reference when migrating an independent XR config to Lua; the RPM never
overwrites your entry point.

The WiVRn RPM is host-only. Headset camera or raw-microphone capture requires a
commit-matched custom HypXR APK; the stock WiVRn client supports neither path.
See /usr/share/doc/wivrn-hypxr/wivrn-hypxr-fedora.md before requesting device
sources. A stock client can still be used for host-only .hypxrtake recording.

This WiVRn build deliberately has no x264 software fallback. Fedora's
ffmpeg-free exposes VAAPI and NVENC encoders, but the machine still needs at
least one working VAAPI, NVENC, or experimental Vulkan Video backend. VAAPI is
the normal AMD/Intel path: H.264/H.265 normally need RPM Fusion's
mesa-va-drivers-freeworld on AMD or its full intel-media-driver on Intel.
NVIDIA needs the proprietary driver 565.77 or newer for NVENC, and AMD should
use Mesa RADV rather than AMDVLK/AMDGPU-PRO. Diagnose with `vainfo`,
`ffmpeg -hide_banner -encoders`, and `nvidia-smi` as applicable.
Because the XR launcher selects hypxrva only for the compositor, set WiVRn's
real GPU device, VA driver, and library path in a per-user wivrn.service drop-in
when the machine requires an override.

The voice and HUD battery services can be enabled for the user with:

  systemctl --user enable --now hypxrvoiced.service hypxrhud-battery.service

hypxrhud itself is also D-Bus activated. The XR config normally starts
wivrn.service only for the XR session instead of enabling it globally.

The keystroke and command-ticker HUD producers are static, privacy-visible
filming aids. Start them manually with `systemctl --user start
hypxrhud-keys.service` or `hypxrhud-cmdlog.service`. Keystrokes require the
external /usr/bin/showmethekey-cli backend. The XR session's private hyprctl is
/usr/libexec/hypxrland/hyprctl; preserve it as HYPXR_CMD_HUD_REAL when opting
into the ticker shim. Full Fedora guidance is installed at
/usr/share/doc/hypxrhud/hypxrhud-fedora.md.

XREAL Air users can additionally install monado-xreal. Its runtime manifest is
/usr/share/openxr/1/openxr_monado-xreal.json and its private user unit is
monado-xreal.service. GPU selection and direct-mode connector settings remain
machine-specific systemd user drop-ins. The RPM requires Fedora's xr-hardware
udev rules; reconnect the glasses after first installation so seat access is
applied.
